## When to Stop Self-Treating and See a Doctor

Some cases of <strong>nail fungus treatment</strong> are appropriate to manage with OTC products. The threshold for seeing a doctor is clearer than most sources make it.

See a doctor if there has been no visible improvement after 3 months of consistent OTC use. Three months is sufficient time to observe some response in superficial infections; if nothing has changed, the infection is almost certainly subungual and OTC treatment will not clear it. See a doctor immediately if more than one nail is affected, if the nail is painful, if the infection appears to be spreading to surrounding skin, or if you have diabetes or a condition that affects circulation or immunity. For these patients, fungal nail infections carry a higher risk of secondary bacterial infection and should not be self-managed. See a doctor if you are uncertain whether the nail change is fungal at all. Nail psoriasis, nail trauma, and contact reactions from gel or acrylic products can produce discolouration and thickening that closely resembles onychomycosis. Treating a non-fungal nail condition with antifungals delays the correct diagnosis and may cause unnecessary side effects. The <a href="https://www.drugs.com/medical-answers/best-over-counter-nail-fungus-treatment-3564243/">clinical overview of OTC vs prescription nail fungus options</a> reinforces that confirmation of diagnosis before escalating to prescription treatment is standard clinical practice.

A GP can confirm diagnosis via nail clipping culture, which takes 2 to 4 weeks to return results but ensures treatment is targeted to the correct organism. A podiatrist can perform nail debridement, which reduces nail thickness and improves both topical penetration and visual outcome during treatment. Neither step is a barrier to getting help. They are the steps that make treatment work.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## Can Nail Fungus Come Back After Treatment

Yes. Recurrence after successful <strong>nail fungus treatment</strong> is common, not a sign of treatment failure. Reported recurrence rates vary between 10 and 50 percent, depending on the study, the treatment type, and follow-up duration. This is not an argument against treating; it is an argument for treating well and then managing conditions afterward.

Recurrence happens through two routes. Reinfection occurs when the same or a different fungal source reaches a previously cleared nail, often from footwear that was not disinfected during treatment, from communal areas such as gym changing rooms or public pools, or from athlete's foot that was not simultaneously cleared. Relapse, as opposed to reinfection, occurs when fungal elements that were not fully eliminated resume growth, particularly in patients who stopped treatment early or whose immune response is compromised.

Prevention after clearance is straightforward: wear moisture-wicking socks, alternate shoes to allow full drying between uses, treat footwear with antifungal powder or spray, protect feet in communal wet areas, and treat any athlete's foot promptly before it reaches the nail. These habits are not complicated. Maintaining them consistently is what separates people who stay clear from those who cycle through treatment repeatedly.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## Nail health is a long game. Play it with clear information.

The treatment for nail fungus is not mysterious. OTC for mild, early, superficial infection. Prescription topical for mild to moderate infection without lunula involvement, or where oral medication is contraindicated. Oral terbinafine for moderate to severe infection, multi-nail involvement, or cases where OTC has already failed. Laser as a possible adjunct, not a replacement. Doctor involvement when you are not sure of the diagnosis, when symptoms are spreading, or when OTC has not worked within 3 months.

What the best outcomes have in common is not the treatment chosen. It is the decision made with accurate information rather than with a product promise. At Mirellé, we consider honest health guidance to be as much part of nail care as any aesthetic choice. Knowing what actually works, and why, is the only way to make a decision you can commit to for the months it takes to see results.

The nail that grows back clear is worth the patience. Start with the right information, choose the right tier, and see it through.
